---
description: Collaboration discipline — align before consequential work, decide-and-recommend, prove don't assert
alwaysApply: true
---

# Working with this codebase

- Dig deep before consequential / design / security work. Context → problem → impact →
  recommendation. Present the plan before writing structural code.
- Decide and recommend. No option menus. Combine, don't choose. Ask once only for a fork the
  maintainer must own.
- Ask before decisive outward actions: merge, close an issue, cut a release, force-push shared
  refs, change repo settings. Merging is the maintainer's.
- Stay on the current target. When redirected, drop the prior thread.
- Retract a wrong fact the moment you find it.
- Prove, don't assert. "Tests pass" is not proof of a behavior claim. Match language to evidence.
- Tests are the spec. Never remove a test to go green. Update a test only when the change
  affected it; improve it (pin the new risk), don't edit a fixture so the code passes.
- A withdrawn fix leaves its tests behind as characterisation.
- Read the CI log before touching a red check. Every failure is a real defect until proven
  otherwise.
- Mutate the line that implements a property and confirm the suite fails. A surviving mutation
  means the test does not cover it, or the code is dead.
- Verify state from the repository (`git show origin/main:<path>`), never from memory or push
  output. Confirm an issue number by reading it back before linking it.
- Work a change surfaces belongs in that change. File only what needs a decision someone else
  must make.
- Search this repo's issues before building. Do not re-implement what already shipped.
